"Weak Hippchen conjecture for highly connected graphs — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/347518/weak-hippchen-conjecture-for-highly-connected-graphs)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Weak Hippchen conjecture for highly connected graphs ... Weak Hippchen conjecture.\n\nWeak Hippchen conjecture for highly connected graphs\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $G$G be a $k$k-connected graph on $n$n vertices. Weak Hippchen conjecture. If $k \\geq n/4$k≥n/4, then every pair of longest paths intersect in at least $k$k vertices. This weakens Hippchen's conjecture and follows the paper's bound when $k \\geq n/4$k≥n/4; it remains open in the source.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Juan Gutiérrez, “On the intersection of two longest paths in k-connected graphs”, arXiv:2008.02163 (2020).\n\nconnectivitygraph-theorylongest-paths\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOWR-2019-13 (https://oa.tib.eu/renate/bitstreams/fb0a16b9-380f-4ce5-89b8-b9d91144cf33/download)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; GENERALIZED HAMADA CONJECTURE: A t-(v, k, λ)q subspace design D, regarded as combinatorial design, has parameters 2-([v 1]q, [k 1]q, λ). ... *Design Theory*, volume 1,2.\n802                                                                 Oberwolfach Report 13/2019\n\nThe advantage of taking a subspace design with small λ over the trivial design is the reduced complexity of the decoder. For every position of a received word, the decoder runs through those r blocks of the design which contain the corresponding point. Therefore, subspace designs with small values of λ are preferable and the trivial subspace design is the worst choice since it attains the maximal value of λ.\n\nThe same can be concluded for the affine designs from subspace designs.\n\nThe above considerations lead to the\n\nGENERALIZED HAMADA CONJECTURE: A t-(v, k, λ)q subspace design D, regarded as combinatorial design, has parameters 2-([v 1]q, [k 1]q, λ). The p-rank of D is minimal among all combinatorial designs with the same parameters.\n\nREFERENCES\n\n[1] T. Beth, D. Jungnickel, and H. Lenz. *Design Theory*, volume 1,2. Cambridge University Press, second edition, 1999.  \n[2] M. Braun, M. Kiermaier, and A. Wassermann. q-analogs of designs: Subspace designs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Euler-symmetric projective varieties (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318652927_Euler-symmetric_projective_varieties)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; By Theorem 3.7, every Euler-symmetric projective variety Z is an equivariant compactiﬁcation ... On the basis of Theorem 5.5, we propose the following, which would imply Conjecture 5.1. ... FH14 , Uniqueness of equivariant compactiﬁcations of C n by a Fano manifold of Picard number 1,\nwhich is linearly normal.\n\n5. Equivariant compactiﬁcations of vector groups\n\nBy Theorem 3.7, every Euler-symmetric projective variety Z is an equivariant compactiﬁcation\n\nof a vector group; that is, there exists an action of the vector group W, where dim W= dim Z,\n\non Z with an open orbit. Is the converse also true? In [HT99, Section 4.1], it is pointed out\n\nthat there exists an equivariant compactiﬁcation of G 1\n\na, a singular curve, which does not admit\n\nG 1\n\na-equivariant projective embeddings. By [Baz13], there are many singular cubic hypersurfaces\n\nwhich are equivariant compactiﬁcations of vector groups, but by Example 3.14, they are not\n\nEuler symmetric. Thus it seems reasonable to exclude singular varieties. We propose the following\n\nconjecture.\n\nConjecture 5.1.Let X be a Fano manifold of Picard number 1 which is an equivariant com-\n\npactiﬁcation of a vector group. Then X can